# =============================================================================
# DENY BY DEFAULT.
#
# This working directory is ~425 GB and contains patient-derived genomic data
# for Burkholderia pseudomallei, a US Tier 1 Select Agent. The metadata joins
# accession -> isolation_location -> collection_date -> CDC exposure label,
# which is re-identifiable for rare cases (61 USA rows, 681 rows with a
# populated isolation_location, 16 with a validation label).
#
# This file therefore ignores EVERYTHING and re-admits only source code and
# written documentation, by explicit name or extension, at the top level only.
# There is no rule anywhere below that re-admits a data file. Adding one is a
# decision, not an oversight -- and `git add -f` bypasses all of this, so do not
# use it without checking what you are forcing.
#
# Verify before every push:
#     git ls-files | xargs -r du -ch | tail -1     # total tracked size
#     git ls-files                                  # read the whole list
# =============================================================================

# --- 1. everything, including all subdirectories and their contents ----------
*
!.gitignore

# Subdirectories are NOT re-admitted. Every analysis output, work directory,
# assembly set, bundle and archive lives in one, so excluding them wholesale is
# the single most important rule here. Named for the record:
#   L1v4c_work/ (232G) v4c_local/ (21G) L1v4c_out/ L1v4b_out/ L1v3_out/
#   L1_out/ L1_clean_out/ L1v4b_partition/ null_sim/ fconst_sensitivity/
#   a100_stage/ a100_v4c_partition/ refsens_cluster37/ additions/ spikein/
#   pp2802_out/ cfml/ all35_out/ wfsnps-v4c-results/ prod_*/ fbL1_*/
# To track code from one of them, add an explicit `!path/to/file.py` line and
# say why in the commit message.

# --- 2. re-admit source code (top level only) --------------------------------
!*.py
!*.sh
!*.nf
!*.config
!*.R
!*.pl

# --- 3. re-admit written documentation (top level only) ----------------------
!*.md

# --- 4. re-admit build/CI metadata if it is ever added -----------------------
!Makefile
!environment.yml
!requirements.txt
!LICENSE

# =============================================================================
# 5. BELT AND BRACES.
#
# Everything below is already excluded by rule 1. It is restated so that a
# future `!*.tsv`-style loosening of the allowlist cannot silently start
# tracking isolate data. These rules come after the allowlist, so they win.
# =============================================================================

# --- isolate-level and epidemiological data — never commit -------------------
*.tsv
*.csv
*.xlsx
*.xls
*.json
*.jsonl
*METADATA*
*metadata*
*PANEL_*
*panel_*
*cluster_membership*
*clusters*.tsv
*assignments*
*samplesheet*
rfile*.txt
*_dates.csv
*_genomes.tsv

# --- sequence, alignment and tree data ---------------------------------------
*.fasta
*.fa
*.fna
*.fastq
*.fastq.gz
*.fq
*.gz
*.zst
*.bz2
*.tar
*.aln
*.phylip
*.vcf
*.gff
*.bam
*.sam
*.bed
*.nwk
*.tre
*.tree
*.treefile
*.iqtree
*.skf
*.msh

# --- run logs and workflow state ---------------------------------------------
*.log
.nextflow*
work/
.command.*
trace*.txt
timeline*.html
report*.html
dag*.html
pipeline_info/

# --- session transcripts (contain full conversation history) -----------------
claude-session/
*.jsonl
.claude/

# --- editor, OS and Python noise ---------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
.ipynb_checkpoints/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.idea/
.vscode/

# --- credentials — nothing here today, and nothing here tomorrow -------------
.env
.env.*
*.pem
*.key
*credentials*
*secret*
rclone.conf
.netrc

# --- backups and scratch ------------------------------------------------------
*.bak
*.pre-crlf-fix
*.orig
*.rej
*.tmp
tmp/
scratch/

# --- 6. documentation that embeds per-isolate tables --------------------------
# These read as prose but contain per-isolate QC rows (accession + country +
# species + read stats). That is isolate data regardless of the file extension,
# so they are excluded by name. Docs that merely *cite* an accession while
# making a methodological argument are kept -- e.g. METHODS_DRAFT and FINDINGS
# each show the two ONT genomes' branch lengths, which is the argument itself.
NEW200_QC_REPORT_2026-08-17.md
PACBIO_CLR_DIAGNOSIS_2026-08-17.md
THEIAPROK_SETTINGS.md
READ_SCREEN_RESCUE.md
INTERPRETATION_2026-08-16.md

# ACQUISITION_TARGETS_US table 1 lists five 2021 aromatherapy-outbreak runs as
# run + biosample + month-precision date + anatomical specimen (blood, lung),
# and the prose immediately below names the four patients' US states. That is
# the accession -> date -> location -> specimen join this file exists to
# prevent, on exactly the rare-USA-case population called out at the top. The
# accessions are individually public; the assembled join is the exposure.
ACQUISITION_TARGETS_US_2026-08-21.md

# --- 7. explicit exceptions from subdirectories -------------------------------
# Rule 1 excludes all subdirectories. This is resource configuration, not data:
# no isolate identifiers, and it carries the measured rationale for the tuning
# (see the SNIPPY_SCATTER block). Worth versioning alongside the code it tunes.
# Un-ignoring the directory is required for git to descend into it at all, so
# it is immediately re-denied and each admitted file is named. Without the
# `a100_stage/*` line, a blanket `!a100_stage/` silently admits any future .md
# or .sh dropped in there -- which is how three files appeared unnoticed.
!a100_stage/
a100_stage/*
!a100_stage/curated_L1_overrides_a100.config
!a100_stage/run_a100.sh
!a100_stage/build_bundle.sh
!a100_stage/README_A100.md

# --- 8. the frozen basis and the retirement record ---------------------------
# Documentation, not data: no isolate identifiers in either file. These are the
# two documents that record WHICH partition every reported number belongs to,
# and WHY 200+ files were moved out of the workspace. Left untracked they are
# lost on a fresh clone -- exactly when someone needs them most. Un-ignore each
# directory so git can descend, then immediately re-deny it so that no data file
# inside is ever admitted, and name the one admitted file. Same shape as the
# a100_stage block above, and for the same reason: a blanket `!DIR/` would
# silently start tracking whatever lands in there later.
!FINAL_BASIS_2026-08-22/
FINAL_BASIS_2026-08-22/*
!FINAL_BASIS_2026-08-22/README.md
!RETIRED_2026-08-22/
RETIRED_2026-08-22/*
!RETIRED_2026-08-22/README.md
# collaborator/ records a LIVE recall condition (Yuyi's A100 results, pending as
# of 2026-08-22), so its README is the one place that pending dependency is
# written down. Same narrow pattern; the runbooks themselves stay untracked.
!collaborator/
collaborator/*
!collaborator/README.md

# --- 9. the exclusion register's SPEC (added 2026-08-23) ----------------------
# PANEL_EXCLUSIONS_README.md is caught by the `*PANEL_*` guard in section 1.
# That guard is aimed at isolate-level DATA -- the register TSV itself stays
# ignored, as does every PANEL_*.tsv. This file is the normative spec: it defines
# the cross-check that must be run before a panel build, and (since 2026-08-23)
# the `status=retired` mechanism and the `core=na%` transcription trap.
#
# Untracked, it is lost on a fresh clone -- and losing it is precisely how four
# exclusions that were retired on evidence would get silently re-applied.
#
# It does name four accessions in prose. That is the same thing tracked
# documents already do (MANUSCRIPT_OUTLINE names them too); the line this repo
# draws is between a per-genome data table and a document explaining a decision.
# One file, named explicitly -- no directory is opened up.
!PANEL_EXCLUSIONS_README.md

# rapid ID sketches (regenerable in ~1 min from paths.txt)
rapid_id_*/panel*.msh
rapid_id_*/*.msh
rapid_id_*/dist_raw.tsv
refs_bpc/bpc.zip
refs_bpc/bpc/
refs_bpc/*.msh
refs_bpc/mallei8.zip
refs_bpc/mallei8/
rapid_id_*/cgmlst_refs/out*/cds_coordinates.tsv
rapid_id_*/cgmlst_refs/input*/
